{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"673e7e81ce2ddb546b06955ebfbaffa09dd4817181521eabb7228d627fc4d1b1","slug":"index-13","location":"/chapters/appD_transformers/0_index.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"D - Transformers in Depth","authors":[{"id":"Roger Weber","name":"Roger Weber"}],"content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"0_index.md","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/0_index-a79d982d37c8786134e83e465aa003e9.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transformers appear in most chapters of this book: they produce the embeddings of semantic search, write the answers of retrieval-augmented generation, align images and text, and transcribe speech. The chapters explain what these models compute and how to use them, and they often mention steps that they do not show: a model is “fine-tuned” for a task, a “BERT-based classifier” labels text, a language model “generates” an answer. This chapter shows these steps. It describes the families of transformer models and how they are pretrained, how a pretrained model is fine-tuned for a task, how the classifiers compare on a real text classification task, and how a generative model produces text.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"ZQQWA40Jtf"}],"key":"X1CCaVC0mf"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"seealso","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where this is used","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"JbqgnyXzXm"}],"key":"Xv7yM9whAp"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[